Below is a Python implementation of `check(part)` using only the Python standard library.

### Modeling Approach & Engineering Assumptions
Because the written specification is a **2D reference-area criterion** ("1% in any 10 mm × 10 mm reference area", i.e. $\le 1.0\text{ mm}^2$ of pore area per $100\text{ mm}^2$) evaluated on a **3D CT pore summary table** (without voxel slices or the part's 3D CAD/mesh geometry), the function makes the following explicit modeling choices:

1. **Pore cross-sections (equivalent sphere + Cavalieri lower bound):** Each pore is modeled as a sphere of diameter `d_eq_mm` centered at `centroid`, giving circular cross-sections on any 2D cutting plane. Because flat or planar shrinkage pores (`sphericity < 0.5`) can have larger true cross-sections than an equivalent sphere, the function also applies a mathematically exact Cavalieri lower bound using `volume_mm3` and `bbox` extents along X, Y, and Z.
2. **Plane & window search:** Uses exact 3D spatial pruning, then searches principal axes, diagonals, a Fibonacci hemisphere of oblique plane normals, and local PCA planes for flattened pore clusters—testing 12 in-plane rotations of the $10\text{ mm} \times 10\text{ mm}$ square with exact disk-rectangle intersection area.
3. **Reference area denominator ($100\text{ mm}^2$):** Without the part's 3D surface geometry, the local material cross-sectional area inside a $10\text{ mm} \times 10\text{ mm}$ window cannot be determined, so a full $100\text{ mm}^2$ denominator is used. **Caveat:** In thin walls (`nominal_wall_mm` or `wall_mm < 10 mm`) or near edges where a $10\text{ mm} \times 10\text{ mm}$ window overhangs the part, the actual material area is smaller than $100\text{ mm}^2$, so true local area porosity can be higher than computed here. For borderline parts or highly irregular shrinkage porosity, verify directly on CT cross-sectional slice images.

