A pore belongs to the sealing zone if its `centroid` lies inside or on the boundary of a sealing zone box, and its full `volume_mm3` is counted (rather than using `bbox` overlap or prorated partial volume).
"A part without a zone named 'sealing' has no pores in it" is interpreted to mean the sealing-zone pore volume is zero (0% sealing-zone porosity), so a part without a `'sealing'` zone is accepted regardless of pores elsewhere in the part.
Zone name matching for `'sealing'` is exact and case-sensitive.
If multiple zones are named `'sealing'`, they are combined into a single sealing region using their 3D geometric union so overlapping zone volumes and pores in overlapping areas are not double-counted.
"Must not exceed 0.2%" is treated as an inclusive limit (`porosity <= 0.002`).
All pores in `part["pores"]` within the sealing zone are included regardless of size, sphericity, or whether they are open to the surface (`dist_surface_mm == 0`).
If a `'sealing'` zone has zero volume, the part is accepted only if the sealing-zone pore volume is zero.

In CT porosity acceptance criteria for cast and additively manufactured parts (e.g., VW 50093, VDG P201, ASTM/ISO NDT standards), unqualified "pore size" is conventionally taken as the maximum linear extent (`feret_max_mm`), which is also the conservative choice compared to `d_eq_mm` for non-spherical shrinkage or lack-of-fusion pores.

